// The one surface that stays while the panel window is away.
//
// It answers a question the window cannot answer while it is hidden: is Squid
// running, and what is it doing? The icon says the first, the hover text says
// the second in the status line's own words. It is also what "put away" runs
// on: closing the window hides it, and the tray's menu is how the person gets
// it back or ends the run from there.
//
// **The menu is verified to export on Linux, and the lifecycle leans on that.**
// The opposite was measured once and believed for a while: a stand-in
// `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher` read `GetLayout` back as `(0, (0, {}, []))`
// with `AboutToShow` answering `false`, and Slint 1.17.1 got blamed for an
// empty menu. The stand-in was the culprit — while it was busy reading the
// menu back, it swallowed ksni's `IsStatusNotifierHostRegistered` query
// (jeepney's blocking reply wait drops non-matching inbound messages), so the
// tray's spawn failed, the menu was never exported, and the same stand-in then
// reported the empty menu it had caused. With the stand-in answering before
// reading, the same measurement on the same panel returns both entries below.
// The lesson generalizes: a fake host must answer like a real one first —
// register, respond to the property query — and only then read back.
//
// The menu carries exactly two entries and nothing conditional. **Show Squid**
// is the way back in: the window is never dropped by hiding, so showing it
// again is all "put away" takes to undo. **Quit Squid** is the way out, and
// earns its place by not requiring the window first: quitting without hunting
// for a window behind six others.
//
// Two things deliberately absent. **Pause** stays in the panel's Recording
// section, which owns the prose that goes with it: pause control is FIFO-backed, so
// it is unconfigured on most runs and unavailable on Windows entirely. **A count**
// belongs to the desktop notice — only "Waiting for you" earns a badge in this
// design, and a Windows tray icon cannot carry one.
export component SquidTray inherits SystemTrayIcon {
    // `icon` and `tooltip` belong to `SystemTrayIcon`, and Slint generates Rust
    // accessors only for what the exported component declares — an inherited
    // property has no setter on the binding. So each one gets a declared property
    // here and the inherited one is bound to it. The names differ because they
    // must: a re-declaration would collide with the base rather than override it.
    in property <image> icon-image;
    in property <string> hover-text;
    icon: root.icon-image;
    tooltip: root.hover-text;

    // A literal, unlike the tooltip, because this one is not state: `title` is what
    // accessibility tools read and what some desktops show when they list tray
    // icons. It was empty until a fake StatusNotifier host read the properties back
    // off the running panel. Windows renders only the icon and ignores it; that is
    // no reason to publish nothing on the host where a screen reader is the only
    // way to find this icon at all.
    title: "Squid";

    callback show();
    callback quit();

    Menu {
        MenuItem {
            title: "Show Squid";
            activated => { root.show(); }
        }
        MenuItem {
            title: "Quit Squid";
            activated => { root.quit(); }
        }
    }
}
